 ],
 "literal": "Like the carded cotton of a spinning woman, / the fat-glistening rich pieces of meat whose fire-anger has cooled down, / in large-rimmed bowls, are passed around in turn, / we shall eat, my lord! I have come to see you, / in your festive assembly, O capable man, where the strength of enemies is ruined! / Like a strong ploughed bull eating straw, / you drink with relish the toddy that becomes good nectar. / Even the victor who overcame and killed, causing hill-like male elephants to retreat, speaks of you, / saying, 'He is the victor!' / And even the loser, whose splendid red feet adorned with hero's anklets gripped the ground / as he rushed to withstand the battle, speaks of you, / saying, 'If Malaiyaṉ of the strong spear were not there, / winning this great battle would indeed have been easy for us!' / and 'He is the one who was defeated.' / You became unique, O Lord! To those who obtained you, / you are like Murugan of the high mountain, which is the abundant abode of great rain clouds!",
 "translation": "Like the soft, carded cotton of a spinning woman, the fat-glistening, rich pieces of meat, whose fire-heat has cooled, are passed around in turn in large-rimmed bowls. We shall feast, my lord! I have come to see you in your festive assembly, O capable hero, where the pride of your enemies is broken. Like a strong bull that has finished ploughing and happily eats its feed, you drink with relish the sweet toddy that tastes like nectar. Even the victor who conquered and slew, making mountain-like elephants retreat, speaks of you, saying, 'He is the true victor!' And even the defeated foe, whose splendid red feet adorned with warrior anklets gripped the ground as he rushed to withstand the battle, speaks of you, saying, 'If Malaiyaṉ of the strong spear had not been there, winning this great battle would have been easy for us!' and 'He is the one who was truly defeated.' You have become unique, O Lord! To those who have obtained your support, you are like Murugan of the high mountain, the abundant home of great rain clouds!",
 "modern": "நூல் நூற்கும் பெண்ணின் பஞ்சு போன்ற, நெருப்பின் சூடு தணிந்த, கொழுப்பு மின்னும் கறித்துண்டுகள் பெரிய வாயையுடைய பாத்திரங்களில் முறைமுறையாகப் பரிமாறப்பட, என் தலைவனே, உன்னைக் காண வந்துள்ளோம்; பகைவர்களின் வலிமையை அழித்த வல்லமையுடையவனே, உனது மகிழ்ச்சியான கொடை இருக்கையில் நாம் உண்போம்! உழுது முடித்த வலிமையான எருது வைக்கோலைத் தின்பது போல, நல்ல அமிழ்தமாக நீ விரும்பி உண்ணும் கள் அது! குன்றுகளைப் போன்ற யானைகள் பின்வாங்கி ஓட, போரில் வென்றவனும் உன்னைப் புகழ்ந்து பேசுவான், 'இவனே வென்றவன்' என்று! வீரக்கழல் அணிந்த சிவந்த அடிகள் நிலத்தைப் பற்ற, விரைந்து வந்து போரைத் தாங்கிய வலிமையான வேலைக் கொண்ட மலையன் மட்டும் இல்லையென்றால், இந்தப் போரில் வெல்வது நமக்கு எளிதாக இருந்திருக்கும் என்று கூறி, தோற்றவனும் உன்னைப் புகழ்ந்து பேசுவான், 'அவனே தோற்றவன்' என்று! பெருமழையைத் தரும் மேகங்கள் தங்கும் உயர்ந்த மலையில் வாழும் அழகிய முருகனைப் போல, உன்னைத் துணையாகப் பெற்றவர்களுக்கு நீ ஒருவனே தனிப்பெரும் தலைவனாக விளங்கினாய்!",
 "explanation": "The poet Vaṭamavaṇṇakkaṇ Peruñcāttaṉār addresses the chieftain Tērvaṇ Malaiyaṉ in this poem of the vākai (victory) tiṇai. The poet describes arriving at the chieftain's court, where a lavish feast of tender, fat-glistening meat and sweet toddy is served. He praises Malaiyaṉ's unmatched military prowess by stating that both the victor and the vanquished in battle attribute the final outcome entirely to Malaiyaṉ's intervention. If Malaiyaṉ fights on one side, that side is guaranteed victory, making him the ultimate kingmaker. The comparison of cooked meat to the carded cotton of a spinning woman is a vivid domestic image that highlights the tenderness of the food. The comparison of the chieftain to Murugan on a rain-swept mountain emphasizes his divine-like protection and generosity to his dependents.",
